The combined score is `max_kvpr + execution_time`, so gains must come from lowering max_kvpr without adding runtime. The current local search recomputes `max_kvpr` (summing over all models on all GPUs) for every candidate move/swap — very slow per iteration, meaning few randomized restarts actually run within the 1.5s budget. I propose:

1. **Incremental local search**: maintain per-GPU `load` and `free` arrays, evaluate each candidate move/swap in O(1), only syncing back on improvement. This makes each search pass dramatically cheaper, so far more greedy restarts get refined within the same time budget.
2. **Targeted moves**: prioritize moving models *off the worst GPU*, which is the only move that can lower the max KVPR (moves among non-worst GPUs can't help the objective).

